.TH EXEC 2
.SH NAME
exec \- execute a file
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "vsys:libuser"
.PP
.nf
.B "exec :: proc(path: string, argv: []string = nil) -> i64"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Exec
replaces the calling process's program with the one at
.IR path ,
resolved in the caller's own name space, and transfers to its entry
point.
.I Argv
is the new program's arguments, its own name first by convention.
.PP
The process's pid, its descriptors and its name space survive an
.IR exec ,
which is what lets a shell open a file, redirect it onto a descriptor,
and then
.I exec
the command: the redirect outlives the replace.
The text, data and stack are the new program's; nothing of the old
program's memory remains, except a segment the caller allocated with
.B abi.SEGSHARED
(see
.IR segattach (2)),
which crosses into the new image if the new image does not overlap it.
A note handler registered with
.I notify
does not survive: it names an address in text that is gone, so a note
delivered to a newly exec'ed process is once again only an ending, until
the new program registers a handler of its own.
See
.IR notify (2).
.PP
.I Exec
returns only on failure.
On success there is no return: the calling image is gone, and the value
that would have been returned is never read.
.SH SOURCE
.B sys/libuser/sys.odin
.br
.B kernel/user/exec.odin
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR intro (2),
.IR fork (2),
.IR notify (2),
.IR segattach (2),
.IR stat (2)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
On failure, answers a negative
.B \-errno
and the caller keeps running; see
.IR errstr (2).
